{"id":"respiratory-pharmacology","system":"Respiratory","subject":"Pharmacology","pages":[704,706],"rounds":[{"t":"Decongestants, antihistamines, antitussives and asthma drugs","q":[{"s":"How do pseudoephedrine and phenylephrine relieve nasal congestion?","o":["They block H1 receptors","They stabilize mast cells","They activate β2 receptors","They activate α-adrenergic receptors, causing vasoconstriction in the nasal mucosa","They block muscarinic receptors"],"e":["Wrong. Antihistamines.","Wrong. Cromolyn.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"704","k":"Pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine"},{"s":"A man has used an oxymetazoline-type nasal spray for 2 weeks and now his congestion is worse than before. What is this?","o":["Allergic rhinitis","Sinusitis","Nasal polyps","Rhinitis medicamentosa (rebound congestion)","Serotonin syndrome"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Use limited to 4–6 days.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"704","k":"Pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine"},{"s":"Which adverse effect is a concern with α-agonist decongestants?","o":["Hypertension","Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia","Hypotension","Hyperkalemia"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Opposite.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"704","k":"Pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine"},{"s":"Besides decongestion, what else can pseudoephedrine be used for?","o":["Treating hypertension","Opening obstructed eustachian tubes","Sedation","Treating cough","Treating asthma attacks"],"e":["Wrong. Causes it.","Correct.","Wrong. Causes stimulation.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"704","k":"Pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine"},{"s":"Which drug is a first-generation H1-blocker?","o":["Loratadine","Cetirizine","Fexofenadine","Desloratadine","Diphenhydramine"],"e":["Wrong. Second generation.","Wrong. Second generation.","Wrong. Second generation.","Wrong. Second generation.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"704","k":